  <section id="s-premid" data-code="premid">
    <div class="section-no">02 — Pre vs Mid-Training</div>
    <h2>Pre-Training vs. Mid-Training: Ingesting vs. Specializing</h2>
    <p><strong>Pre-training</strong> is the foundation. The model crawls a massive general-purpose token corpus (e.g. 25–30T tokens) at a short context window (typically 4K tokens) to learn grammar, vocabulary, facts, and basic coding syntax. The optimizer updates all parameters uniformly. This phase eats up to 90% of the project's budget and takes months to run.</p>
    <p><strong>Mid-training</strong> (often called Continued Pre-training) is the specialization step. Once the base model is trained, it undergoes mid-training to bridge the gap between raw web tokens and complex tasks. The context length is scaled progressively (e.g. from 4K $\rightarrow$ 32K $\rightarrow$ 128K $\rightarrow$ 200K $\rightarrow$ 1M tokens), and the data mix shifts. The corpus is heavily packed with repo-level code (including git diffs, pull requests, and file structures) and math textbook proofs, allowing the model to adapt its attention matrices to long-range dependencies without catastrophic forgetting.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s-sft" data-code="sft">
    <div class="section-no">03 — Masked-Loss SFT</div>
    <h2>SFT: Formatting, Thinking, and Masked Loss</h2>
    <p>Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) transforms the specialized base model into an interactive assistant. In SFT, we supply high-quality templates of dialogue, instructions, and step-by-step reasoning traces. SFT introduces crucial modern features:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Interleaved & Preserved Thinking:</strong> Teaching the model to emit a `<think>` block containing step-by-step logic before writing code or calling external tools. Preserved thinking retains these thoughts in multi-turn sandboxes.</li>
      <li><strong>Turn-level control:</strong> Enabling users to toggle reasoning depth per-turn to trade off latency and accuracy.</li>
      <li><strong>Masked-Loss Trajectories:</strong> In agent tasks, SFT data contains complete sandbox execution paths, including errors and correction steps. Rather than masking out errors (which would hide mistake-recovery patterns), we retain the error trajectories but **mask out the loss calculation** on wrong actions. The model observes the error but is only penalized or rewarded for the correct recovery steps, preventing it from reinforcing bad behaviors.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s-rlvr" data-code="rlvr">
    <div class="section-no">04 — RLVR and Verifiers</div>
    <h2>RLVR: Verifiable Rewards vs. Reward Hacking</h2>
    <p>The traditional method for aligning models with human preferences is RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback), where a learned **Reward Model (RM)** scores outputs. While effective for dialogue style, learned reward models are highly susceptible to **reward hacking** — the model learns to write long, polite, and authoritative-looking responses that contain subtle logical or factual errors because the RM is biased towards layout and style. This prevents the emergence of deep reasoning.</p>
    <p><strong>RLVR (Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards)</strong> solves this. Instead of a learned model, RLVR uses **deterministic, rule-based verifiers** — e.g. a Python compiler that runs unit tests, a formal math validator (like SymPy) that evaluates mathematical equivalence, or a compiler checking syntax. The reward is binary: if the generated code passes the unit tests, the reward is `1.0`; if it fails, the reward is `0.0`. This forces the policy to learn **logical correctness** and drives the emergence of autonomous self-correction. If a test fails, the model backtracks, edits its code, and tries again until it succeeds.</p>
    
