    <h3>The Search Compression Debate</h3>
    <p>A major discussion in frontier AI is the **Search Compression** debate. Does RLVR train the model to think "more intelligently" in a general sense? Or is it simply optimizing the model's policy to quickly find paths that satisfy the verifier's strict rules? While the model becomes highly optimized at solving math and programming tasks, it can struggle to transfer this reasoning to domains where deterministic verifiers do not exist, making hybrid systems (combining rule-based and generative reward models) essential.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s-systems" data-code="systems">
    <div class="section-no">05 — Systems & Algorithms</div>
    <h2>Post-Training Systems: GRPO, slime, and Distillation</h2>
    <p>Scaling up reinforcement learning to frontier sizes requires optimized architectures and training systems. Modern pipelines utilize three primary mechanisms:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization):</strong> Replaces PPO by eliminating the heavy critic (value function) model. Instead, it generates a group of $G$ outputs per prompt and calculates advantages by normalizing rewards *relative* to the group mean, saving up to 50% GPU memory.</li>
      <li><strong>Asynchronous execution (e.g. slime):</strong> Naive RL runs rollouts and training synchronously, causing expensive training GPUs to sit idle during rollout generation. Frameworks like Zhipu's `slime` decouple rollout inference nodes from training nodes via a queue, keeping GPUs at 100% load.</li>
      <li><strong>On-Policy Cross-Stage Distillation:</strong> Running sequential RL stages (Reasoning RL $\rightarrow$ Agent RL $\rightarrow$ General RL) causes capability regression. Cross-stage distillation acts as the final anchor, using checkpoints of previous stages as teachers to pull the current policy back via KL divergence.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

